X:1
T:Yn Dyrfa Weddus
T:In a Seemly Throng
R:carol
S:https://amgueddfa.cymru/casgliadau/caneuongwerin/?id=10 (collected by D. Roy Saer)
N:Sung by Watkin Evans (born 1906), who worked for the Liverpool Corporation, of Abertridwr, Llanwddyn, Montgomeryshire; recorded 21.6.63 (St Fagans tape 611). A Christmas carol he learnt as a small boy from his father, a native of Llangadfan, around the time of the First World War. When recording he left out lines 5-8 of verse 1 and lines 9-12 of verse 2; the full verses come from Telyn Nadolig (1847) by John Williams, Bethel, Llanfyllin, where the carol has seven verses, of which these are 1, 5 and 6. The tune is a form of "The Belle Isle March", once much used for Welsh ballads and carols; this one is very close to the version in Caniadau Bethlehem (ed. J. D. Jones, 1857).
N:The notes in bars 1, 5, 21, 23, 25, 41, 46 and 47 that carry the syllables "rhown", "-u", "am", "ei", "-i", "-i", "-i" and "o'n" are in brackets on the score. In bars 9, 11, 29 and 41 the score also gives three plain quavers in place of the quaver and crotchet (or crotchet and quaver) in the second half of the bar, for verses with an extra syllable. Footnotes on the score give other readings for the later verses: in bar 5 (verse 3) the second half is c/c3/2B; bars 10 and 26 (verses 2 and 3) and 42 (verse 3) end GGF instead of GFG; bar 13 (verse 2) has cBA in the second half; bars 23 and 39 (verse 3) end A2G.
M:6/8
